  "image_name": "1eb283c3a016f4a6766e0b9fe500ac2c.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three image-guidance strategies for finding targets at different depths below a tissue surface. Its main message is that near-infrared (NIR) fluorescence works well for superficial targets but loses sensitivity with depth, while combining NIR with another modality extends detection to deeper tissue.\n\nIn the left scenario, using NIR fluorescence alone, only shallow and moderately deep targets are shown as visible, whereas a deeper target is not detected. In the middle, NIR is paired with radioscintigraphy, so superficial targets can be seen by both methods, intermediate targets appear detectable, and the deepest target is still found by the radiographic method even when NIR no longer reaches it. In the right scenario, NIR is combined with ultrasonography, producing a similar pattern: NIR identifies the more superficial targets, while ultrasound can localize a deeper one that fluorescence misses.\n\nThe display row suggests that each modality contributes separate visual information, and the imaging-system row shows the required devices: an NIR camera alone or together with either a radioscintigraphic camera or an ultrasound probe. Overall, the figure illustrates complementary multimodal imaging, with NIR providing surface-sensitive guidance and the added modality improving deeper target localization.",
